  "label": "Metabolite Overload And Thiol Redox",
  "related": [
    "alters-rna-65d1",
    "aging-senescence-a873",
    "promote-topic-0ce2"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "mitochondrial-acid-2d6e",
  "summary": "New preprints converge on relieving metabolite overload—BCAAs, cystine, long-chain acyl intermediates, ceramides, iron—through pharmacologic, genetic and redox routes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the handling of metabolites that become cytotoxic when enzymatic or transport capacity is exceeded, and the interventions that restore flux. In maple syrup urine disease mice, the mTOR inhibitor rapamycin lowers branched-chain amino acid accumulation, reduces neuroinflammation and extends survival. Lipid nanoparticle delivery of both Hadha and Hadhb mRNA reconstitutes hepatic trifunctional protein and fatty acid oxidation in a mouse model, an enzyme-replacement counterpart to the pharmacologic approach. A parallel set addresses thiol and iron chemistry: TXNDC17 acts with glutathione to prevent cystine buildup and cystine-induced death, while the fungal anthraquinone bostrycin targets TXNRD1 and perturbs iron, redox and glutathione metabolism to drive ferroptosis. Lipid-side control appears in the DES1 inhibitor PR280, which raises ceramides broadly with limited specificity. Hepatocyte NDRG1 adds a post-translational layer, promoting GAMT-dependent creatine production that suppresses PHB1 succinylation during acetaminophen injury.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
